  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1212 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nHot temperatures are expected to continue through much of the work \nweek, with the hottest temperatures occurring around mid-week as we \nsee an uptick in heights and thickness values as the upper ridge and \narea of high pressure centers itself in the vicinity of the Caprock \nregions. In combination with breezy southerly surface flow we can \nexpect highs to become hot across the area, in the upper 90s to \nlower triple digits. With the hottest afternoon temperatures fixated \noff the Caprock, inching close to heat advisory level criteria given \n850 mb temperatures around 32C to 35C. A slight change to the upper \nlevel pattern is expected to occur towards the end of the work week \nas an H5 shortwave trough dives into the western CONUS, allowing \nflow aloft across the region to become oriented southwesterly. As a \nresult, geopotential heights along with thickness values will \ndecrease while a modest plume of monsoonal moisture transports \nitself into the region thanks to southwesterly flow at the H3 to H7 \nlayer. Increased moisture across the region will promote the chance \nfor isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms each afternoon \nthrough late evening Thursday through Saturday. Steering flow will \nremain weak, generally less than 20 knots, which will limit \nthunderstorm activity from expanding eastward into areas off the \nCaprock. Storms will likely remain fixated west of the I-27 \ncorridor, as they develop off the higher terrain and slowly make \ntheir way into areas across the Caprock. The severe weather threat \nremains low at this time, however MUCAPE up to 1200 J/kg suggest \nthat an isolated storm or two may have the potential of becoming \nmarginally severe with strong downburst winds given inverted-v \nprofiles.",
